<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Bakar of Johor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Abu Bakar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Bakar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> was temenggung of Johor from 1862, maharaja from 1868 and sultan from 1886. He moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-bahru">Johor Bahru</a>, encouraged Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and gambier planters, and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, cultivating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and the British. In 1895 he gave Johor a written constitution. His diplomacy kept Johor outside the Federated Malay States, and he is remembered as the father of modern Johor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-bakar-of-johor.jpg" alt="Abu Bakar of Johor" width="250" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Mr Tan at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johor-AbuBakar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
